      "id": 1,
      "text": "Wallace was originally known as Unthank before being renamed in 1921 to honor Secretary of Agriculture Henry Wallace.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://nearbylens.com/cities/usa/wv/wallace/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town of Wallace is located in the Appalachian Mountains of southern West Virginia, surrounded by rolling hills and lush forests.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://nearbylens.com/cities/usa/wv/wallace/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Wallace was once a thriving coal mining town and now celebrates that heritage with an annual Coal Camp Days festival.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://nearbylens.com/cities/usa/wv/wallace/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Nobel laureate mathematician John Nash, portrayed in the film A Beautiful Mind, once lived in Wallace as a child.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://nearbylens.com/cities/usa/wv/wallace/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The historic McDowell County Courthouse in Wallace was built in 1892 and is listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
